# Module Slides

When asked to make slides for a module, build one Markdown deck from **all** assigned reading for that module. Do not skip chapters or their sections that exist in the repo.

## Sources (in priority order)

1. **Module assignment** — `Modules/module readings.md` (which chapter folders and any partial `04Types` notes apply).
2. **Chapter content** — every section `.md` file in those numbered chapter folders, except `README.md`. Sort by filename/number.
3. **Custom content** — `Tutorials/` or other supplements only when listed for the module. **Prefer and emphasize textbook chapters** over tutorials or external notes.

If a chapter folder is listed but has no section files yet, include a single placeholder slide noting the topic is coming soon.

### Partial `04Types`

- **through Type Aliases** — `04Types/01` through `04Types/08`
- **Enums and Structs** — `04Types/09` through `04Types/13`

## Output

- **Path:** `Modules/Slides (Module X).md` (replace `X` with the module number).
- **Format:** Marp-style slides separated by `---` on its own line.
- **Style:** Simple and short. One main idea per slide. Minimal bullets (usually 3–6). Short phrases, not paragraphs.

Follow the textbook voice rules in `textbook-md-chapters.mdc` (no em/en dashes as punctuation, clear plain language).

## Deck flow (in order)

### 1. Title slide

```markdown
# Module X - Module Name
## Programming II
### CPSI 27603
#### University of Arkansas at Little Rock
```

Use the exact module number and title from `Modules/module readings.md`.

---

### 2. Review (1–2 slides)

- Look at **previous** modules in `module readings.md`.
- Pick a few ideas students often confuse or forget.
- At least **1** slide, at most **2**.
- Do not re-teach full prior modules; quick reminders only.

---

### 3. Learning Objectives

- Turn this module’s topics into **measurable** objectives (“Explain…”, “Use…”, “Write…”).
- Usually one slide; split only if the list is long.

---

### 4. Key Terms

- Collect important terms from **all** assigned reading (definitions, types, functions, patterns).
- One or two slides. Term + brief definition per bullet.

---

### 5. Content

- Walk through **all** assigned chapter material in a logical order (match section sequence within each chapter unless a clearer story exists).
- **Do not omit** major blocks from any assigned section file.
- Prefer section headings and teaching points over tutorial quiz wording.
- Many short slides beat few crowded slides.
- Code on a slide: one small snippet or signature line when it helps; avoid full listings.
- Use `> NOTE:` / `> PREFERENCE:` ideas as short callout bullets when they matter for class.

---

### 6. Do's and Don'ts

- One slide (or two if needed).
- **Do:** practices the chapters encourage (e.g. use `.at()`, brace style, test after small changes).
- **Don't:** common mistakes warned about in the reading (undefined behavior, off-by-one, unsafe `[]`, etc.).
- Ground every item in this module’s content.
- Don't double list.  Bad example: Do format. Don't forget to format

---

### 7. Key Takeaways

- One slide summarizing what students should remember after the module.

---

### 8. Further Explorations

- One slide suggesting optional follow-ups that build on this module (tools, chapters ahead, small projects). Not required homework unless the user asks.

## Checklist before finishing

- [ ] Every assigned section file was reflected in the Content section.
- [ ] Chapters weighted over tutorials/custom material.
- [ ] Slides separated with `---`.
- [ ] File named `Slides (Module X).md` in `Modules/`.
- [ ] Deck is short and scannable (no walls of text).
